# The vault is data, not source. HQ's serializer writes LF and diffs file content byte-for-byte to
# decide what changed — so if git checked these out as CRLF (the Windows default, core.autocrlf=true),
# every file would read back different from what HQ would write, and the first save of the session
# would rewrite all ~53 files instead of the one you touched. Every checkout would re-trigger it.
#
# Pin it. Line endings in the vault are LF, everywhere, always.
vault/** text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the fixtures the round-trip tests compare byte-for-byte.
*.md text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
